 ## Background
-The integration of SLAM with 3D Gaussian Splatting leverages the explicit geometry representation provided by 3DGS to alleviate misalignment between different viewpoints, leading to better reconstruction compared to NeRF-based methods [S1]. The real-time rendering feature of 3DGS makes neural-based SLAM methods more applicable for robotics tasks such as relocalization, navigation, 6D pose estimation, multi-sensor calibration, and manipulation [S1]. Early approaches combined classical SLAM systems like ORB-SLAM with 3DGS for offline or semi-online training [S2, unofficial]. More recent developments include hybrid neural field SLAM systems like CP-SLAM, PIN-SLAM, and MNE-SLAM, which address challenges in multi-agent cooperation, loop closure, and distributed mapping [S3]. Active reconstruction algorithms also utilize radiance fields for planning and mapping [S4, unofficial].
+The integration of SLAM with 3D Gaussian Splatting leverages the explicit geometry representation provided by 3DGS, which enables flexible reprojection to alleviate misalignment between different viewpoints, leading to better reconstruction compared to NeRF-based methods [S1]. The real-time rendering feature of 3DGS also makes neural-based SLAM methods more applicable for robotics tasks such as relocalization, navigation, 6D pose estimation, multi-sensor calibration, and manipulation [S1]. Early works like CG-SLAM improved reconstruction quality by introducing uncertainty maps based on rendered depth [S1]. Combined approaches with classical SLAM systems like ORB-SLAM demonstrated that while classical methods may achieve lower ATE, unified 3DGS-based methods offer competitive rendering metrics and better integration with the Gaussian representation [S2, unofficial]. Hybrid neural field approaches (e.g., CP-SLAM, PIN-SLAM, HERO-SLAM) have also been developed to address challenges in multi-agent scenarios, loop closure, and semantic consistency, often using point-based or grid+MLP representations [S3]. Active reconstruction algorithms using radiance fields (e.g., ActiveRMAP) utilize information gain and entropy volume to guide camera motion for efficient mapping [S4, unofficial].
 
